    {
      "id": "GENUS_PTEROCLES",
      "name": "Pterocles",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Sandgrouse — medium-sized, ground-dwelling birds of arid and semi-arid regions. Despite their pigeon-like appearance, they are in their own order. Their most remarkable adaptation is the ability of males to absorb water in their belly feathers and carry it to their chicks at nests that can be kilometres from the nearest water source. Their cryptic plumage blends perfectly with desert sand and gravel.",
      "lineage": "Sandgrouse",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "PTEROCLES_ALCHATA",
          "name": "Pterocles alchata",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "Pin-tailed Sandgrouse",
          "lineage": "Sandgrouse",
          "continents": [
            "Europe",
            "Africa",
            "Asia"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 2,
          "description": "The pin-tailed sandgrouse (Pterocles alchata) is a medium large bird in the sandgrouse family. It has a small, pigeon-like head and neck and a sturdy, compact body. It has long pointed wings, which are white underneath, a long tail and a fast direct flight. Flocks fly to watering holes at dawn. The call is a loud kattar-kattar. This gregarious species breeds on dry open treeless plains and similar habitats. Its nest is a ground scrape into which two or three cream-coloured eggs with cryptic markings are laid. Both sexes incubate the eggs.\nThe pin-tailed sandgrouse is about long. Its head and upperparts are yellowish-green. The underparts are white with a chestnut breast band separating the belly from the green neck. Sexes are somewhat similar, but the female is better camouflaged and has a shorter tail than the male. There are two subspecies; P. A. Alchata breeds in southern Europe and P. A. Caudacutus breeds in northwestern Africa, the Middle East and southeastern Asia. It is a partial migrant, with some Asian birds moving to the Middle East and northern Pakistan in winter. Males of the eastern race have duller underparts than the European birds, and the females have white, rather than yellow, wing coverts.",
          "sourcedFrom": "wikipedia",
          "wikipediaTitle": "Pin-tailed sandgrouse",
          "children": [],
